            "en": "A §38 and B §6.2 describe deterrence as discouraging unwanted behaviour. A visible guard, camera or warning can signal scrutiny and consequences. In this authored facility example, the intended outcome of a visible camera is that a person decides not to attempt an unauthorised entry. That is not the same as a locked barrier physically preventing passage, and it does not prove that the camera is monitored or recording. If recordings are reviewed, detective work is also present; if a guard stops an entry, a preventive action is present. Visibility alone provides no guarantee that every person is deterred. A sign can also communicate instructions, so its directive role may coexist with deterrence. Explain the intended behavioural effect and the operational limits rather than relying on the object name. Different functions can exist within the same installation.",
